Section A: Introduction to SELinux 
1. What is SELinux? 
Ans. SELinux stands for Security-Enhanced Linux and provides mandatory 
access control. 

2. What is the main purpose of SELinux? 
Ans. It protects the Linux system by controlling access 
to files and resources. 

3. What is Mandatory Access Control (MAC)? Ans. MAC is a security mechanism that controls 
access according to predefined security policies. 

4. What are the three modes of SELinux? Ans. Enforcing, Permissive, and Disabled. 

5. What is Enforcing mode? Ans. It applies security policies and blocks unauthorized actions. 

6. What is Permissive mode? Ans. It records policy violations but does not block the actions. 

7. What is Disabled mode? Ans. It means SELinux is not active on the system. 

8. Which SELinux mode provides maximum security enforcement? Ans. Enforcing mode. 

9. Which mode is useful for troubleshooting SELinux issues? Ans. Permissive mode. 

10. Is SELinux enabled by default in Ubuntu? Ans. Ubuntu normally uses AppArmor instead of 
SELinux. 

Section B: SELinux Commands 

11. What is the purpose of the `sestatus` command? Ans. It displays detailed information about 
SELinux status. 

12. What does the `getenforce` command do? Ans. It displays the current SELinux mode. 

13. What is the purpose of the `setenforce` command? Ans. It temporarily changes the SELinux 
enforcement mode. 

14. What does `sudo setenforce 0` do? Ans. It changes SELinux from Enforcing to Permissive 
mode. 

15. What does `sudo setenforce 1` do? Ans. It changes SELinux to Enforcing mode. 

16. Can `setenforce` enable SELinux if it is Disabled? Ans. No, it cannot enable SELinux when 
it is Disabled. 

17. Why do we use `sudo` with setenforce? Ans. It provides the administrator privileges required 
to change SELinux mode. 

18. Is the change made by setenforce permanent? Ans. No, it is temporary and normally lasts 
until the next reboot. 

19. What does `ls -l` display? Ans. It displays file permissions, ownership, size, and modification 
time. 

20. What is the purpose of `ls -Z`? Ans. It displays the SELinux security context of a file. 

Section C: Web Directory and File Management 

21. What is the purpose of `/var/www/html`? Ans. It is a common default document-root 
directory for web server files. 

22. Which command creates a web directory? Ans. `sudo mkdir -p /var/www/html` 

23. What does the `mkdir` command do? Ans. It creates a new directory in Linux. 

24. What is the purpose of the `-p` option in mkdir? Ans. It creates parent directories if required 
and avoids errors if the directory already exists. 

25. Which command creates a sample HTML file? Ans. `echo "Welcome to BCA Linux Lab" | 
sudo tee /var/www/html/index.html` 

26. What is the purpose of the `echo` command? Ans. It displays or sends text as output. 

27. What does the `tee` command do? Ans. It writes input into a file and also displays it on the 
terminal. 

28. Why is `sudo tee` used instead of simple output redirection? Ans. Because sudo applies to 
tee, allowing the file to be written with administrator privileges. 

29. What is the purpose of the index.html file? Ans. It is commonly used as the default 
homepage of a website. 

30. What does `ls -l /var/www/html/index.html` show? Ans. It displays the standard Linux 
permissions and details of the file. 
Section D: SELinux Security Contexts 

31. What is an SELinux security context? Ans. It is a security label assigned to a file, directory, 
or process. 

32. What is the purpose of `ls -Zd`? Ans. It displays the SELinux context of a directory itself. 

33. What is the general format of an SELinux context? Ans. It commonly follows the format 
user:role:type:level. 

34. What does `system_u` represent in an SELinux context? Ans. It represents an SELinux user 
identity assigned by policy. 

35. What does `object_r` represent? Ans. It represents an SELinux role commonly used for files 
and other objects. 

36. What does `httpd_sys_content_t` represent? Ans. It is an SELinux type commonly used for 
web content that a web server may read. 

37. What does `s0` represent in an SELinux context? Ans. It represents the SELinux sensitivity 
level. 

38. Are Linux file permissions and SELinux labels the same? Ans. No, they are two separate 
layers of access control. 

39. Can SELinux block access even when Linux permissions allow it? Ans. Yes, SELinux can 
block access if its security policy denies the action. 

40. Why do we inspect the SELinux context of a web directory? Ans. To verify that files have the 
expected security labels. 
Section E: Restore Context and Troubleshooting 

41. What is the purpose of the `restorecon` command? 
Ans. It restores the default SELinux 
security context according to the installed policy. 

42. What does `sudo restorecon -v` do? 
Ans. It restores the expected file context and displays 
the changes made. 

43. What does the `-v` option mean? 
Ans. It stands for verbose and displays detailed 
information about changes. 

44. What does the `-R` option mean in restorecon? 
Ans. It applies the operation recursively to 
directories and their contents. 

45. What is the purpose of `sudo restorecon -Rv /var/www/html`? 
Ans. It recursively restores the 
expected SELinux contexts of the web directory and its contents. 

46. What does `command not found` mean? 
Ans. It means the required command or utility may 
not be installed or available. 

47. Why might `getenforce` show Disabled? 
Ans. SELinux is disabled on the system. 

48. Why might `setenforce` show Permission Denied? 
Ans. The user may not have sufficient 
administrator privileges. 

49. Which Linux distributions commonly support SELinux?  
Ans. Fedora, Rocky Linux, AlmaLinux, and Red Hat Enterprise Linux (RHEL)
